<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Volos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Volos is a port city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, at the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pagasetic-gulf">Pagasetic Gulf</a> below Mount Pelion, and the capital of the Magnesia regional unit. It stands near the sites of ancient Iolkos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pagasae">Pagasae</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrias">Demetrias</a>. Part of Greece since 1881, it grew into an industrial and commercial centre and an important port.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/volos.jpg" alt="Volos" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: konstantinos kolimpa… · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VOLOS-GREECE_-_panoramio_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Volos lies on the Greek mainland about 170 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and 140 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>, at the innermost point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pagasetic-gulf">Pagasetic Gulf</a> and at the foot of Mount Pelion, which Greek tradition made the land of the Centaurs. It is the only outlet to the sea from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>. West of the city are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> settlements of Dimini and Sesklo; Sesklo has the remains of the oldest acropolis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, dated to 6000 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrias">Demetrias</a> was founded in 293 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-i-of-macedon">Demetrius Poliorcetes</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>. Iolcus was known in myth as the home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason">Jason</a>, who sailed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argo</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argonauts">Argonauts</a> in quest of the Golden Fleece. The mound of Kastro in western Volos was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> settlement, with a Mycenaean palace complex where a few <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablets have been found. The Slavic tribe of the Belegezites settled in the area in the 7th century.</p><p>The city reappears in 1333 under the name Golos, captured by the Byzantine general John Monomachos; the name is of Slavic origin and is thought to come from a word for barren. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> fell under Serbian rule in 1348, returned to Byzantine rule for about twenty years after 1373, and was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a>. Volos was definitively Ottoman only in 1423. The Venetians under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-morosini">Francesco Morosini</a> took the fortress in 1665, but the Ottomans recovered it.</p><p>In April 1827 the Greek fleet under the philhellene Frank Abney Hastings captured five Ottoman ships in the harbour and forced the garrison out, yet the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-constantinople-1832">Treaty of Constantinople</a> of 1832 left Volos outside the new Greek state. It joined the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in November 1881 with the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>, when it had only 4,900 people, and grew rapidly as merchants, craftsmen and sailors moved in. Refugees arrived in the 1920s from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>, Pontus, Cappadocia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-thrace">Eastern Thrace</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> occupation the local rabbi, Moshe Pesach, and the Greek authorities saved about 700 members of the Jewish community from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Volos is the capital of the Magnesia regional unit and the seat of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>, founded in 1984. It had 85,803 people in 2021. Much of the city was rebuilt after catastrophic earthquakes in 1955, and some neoclassical buildings of the late 19th and early 20th centuries survive, among them the Archaeological Museum of Volos of 1909. It is a major port, with ferry connections to the Sporades islands, and an industrial centre known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> and cement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Volos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Iolkos lies at modern <b>Volos</b>, on the Pagasetic Gulf, the best harbour of Thessaly.</blockquote><a href="/state/mycenaean-kingdom-of-iolkos">Mycenaean Kingdom of Iolkos</a></li></ul></section>
